WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.553
Welcome to episode thirteen,
our first exam workshop.

00:00:03.593 --> 00:00:08.706
Today we'll work through two longer
practice questions from start to finish,

00:00:08.746 --> 00:00:11.214
the way you'd tackle them in an exam.

00:00:11.254 --> 00:00:16.678
They're original questions written for
this series, not official exam questions.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:19.610
First, two quick retrieval checks.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:25.764
A muesli bar label says one thousand six
hundred and fifty kilojoules per hundred

00:00:25.804 --> 00:00:26.220
grams.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:30.018
How much energy is in a
forty point zero gram bar?

00:00:30.058 --> 00:00:35.793
And in a catalytic heater, a platinum
catalyst lets methane react at a lower

00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:36.704
temperature.

00:00:36.744 --> 00:00:41.643
Does the catalyst change the energy
released per mole of methane?

00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:42.974
Pause and answer.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.705
The bar: one thousand
six hundred and fifty,

00:00:56.745 --> 00:01:02.559
times forty point zero over one hundred,
is six hundred and sixty kilojoules.

00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:05.296
Scale by the mass you actually have.

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:06.893
And the catalyst: no.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:12.367
It provides a pathway with a lower
activation energy, but the reactants,

00:01:12.407 --> 00:01:17.652
the products and their states are
unchanged, so delta H is unchanged.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.102
Here's the routine we'll
use for every question.

00:01:22.142 --> 00:01:24.316
Read the whole question first.

00:01:24.356 --> 00:01:29.629
Annotate it: units, conditions, and
any words that change the chemistry.

00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:32.360
Choose a method before you calculate.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.312
Work one quantity at a time, with units.

00:01:35.352 --> 00:01:38.894
And finally, check that
the answer is plausible.

00:01:39.266 --> 00:01:43.251
Markers can only award
marks for what they can see.

00:01:43.291 --> 00:01:48.300
Each step that shows a correct
idea, such as an amount in moles,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.088
a balanced equation or a
correctly set-up ratio,

00:01:52.128 --> 00:01:58.321
is an observable step that can earn a
mark, even if something later goes wrong.

00:01:58.361 --> 00:02:01.635
The marks you'll see today are indicative:

00:02:01.675 --> 00:02:06.883
they show how a typical marking
scheme might divide up a question.

00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:11.804
Practice question Q twenty-five
is worth fifteen marks.

00:02:11.844 --> 00:02:17.517
It's an integrated question about a water
heater that burns purified gas from a

00:02:17.557 --> 00:02:18.168
digester.

00:02:18.208 --> 00:02:22.475
Read it all the way through
now, before you write anything.

00:02:22.866 --> 00:02:26.757
Pause the video now and
attempt every part on paper.

00:02:26.797 --> 00:02:30.235
Give yourself about
fifteen to twenty minutes.

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:36.131
If you keep the video running instead,
the next minute is quiet thinking time:

00:02:36.171 --> 00:02:39.724
start by listing what's
given and what's asked.

00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:44.201
Let's read it the way a
careful student would.

00:03:44.241 --> 00:03:48.014
First, the fuel: six point
two zero litres at SLC,

00:03:48.054 --> 00:03:52.819
so we'll convert using twenty-four
point eight litres per mole.

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:54.268
But it's a mixture.

00:03:54.308 --> 00:03:58.233
Only ninety point zero
percent by volume is methane.

00:03:58.273 --> 00:04:04.563
For gases at the same temperature and
pressure, volume percent equals mole percent,

00:04:04.603 --> 00:04:07.882
so ninety percent of
the moles are methane.

00:04:08.266 --> 00:04:09.329
Next, the air.

00:04:09.369 --> 00:04:14.133
Sixty point zero litres of air
is not sixty litres of oxygen.

00:04:14.173 --> 00:04:18.150
Only twenty-one point zero
percent of it is oxygen.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.159
That's worth underlining.

00:04:20.533 --> 00:04:26.196
The enthalpy of combustion is negative
eight hundred and ninety kilojoules per

00:04:26.236 --> 00:04:32.044
mole, for formation of liquid water, which
tells us the states for the equation.

00:04:32.084 --> 00:04:38.039
The water being heated has a mass of eight
hundred point zero grams and warms from

00:04:38.079 --> 00:04:42.060
seventeen point zero to
fifty-seven point zero degrees:

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.659
a rise of forty point zero degrees.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.958
Finally, the carbon dioxide in
the fuel passes through unchanged.

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:55.571
So the carbon dioxide leaving the
heater has two sources: the combustion,

00:04:55.611 --> 00:04:56.955
and the inlet gas.

00:04:56.995 --> 00:05:02.685
Now we know what's asked and what could
trip us up, we can choose methods.

00:05:03.666 --> 00:05:07.664
Before calculating, plan
a method for each part.

00:05:07.704 --> 00:05:12.964
Part a: amounts from volumes at
SLC, then the methane fraction.

00:05:13.004 --> 00:05:17.339
Part b: a balanced equation
with states and delta H.

00:05:17.379 --> 00:05:23.649
Part c: the mole ratio from the equation,
then the excess oxygen as a mass.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:30.716
Part d: energy released from the moles of
methane, and heat gained from m c delta T.

00:05:30.756 --> 00:05:34.081
Part e: efficiency as output over input.

00:05:34.121 --> 00:05:38.540
Part f: new plus inlet carbon
dioxide, then a volume.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.366
And part g: a definition
based on the source.

00:05:42.733 --> 00:05:45.578
Notice that the plan is a chain.

00:05:45.618 --> 00:05:50.536
Part a feeds parts c, d and
f, and part d feeds part e.

00:05:50.576 --> 00:05:53.601
That's why an early error spreads,

00:05:53.641 --> 00:05:59.411
and why it's worth checking part
a carefully before you move on.

00:06:00.398 --> 00:06:00.891
Part a.

00:06:00.931 --> 00:06:07.220
The total amount of fuel mixture is six
point two zero divided by twenty-four point

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:10.117
eight: zero point two five zero moles.

00:06:10.157 --> 00:06:15.911
Methane is ninety point zero percent of
that: zero point two two five moles.

00:06:15.951 --> 00:06:21.858
For oxygen, twenty-one point zero percent
of sixty point zero litres is twelve

00:06:21.898 --> 00:06:28.034
point six litres, and twelve point six
divided by twenty-four point eight is zero

00:06:28.074 --> 00:06:30.169
point five zero eight moles.

00:06:30.209 --> 00:06:33.106
Three observable steps, one mark each.

00:06:33.464 --> 00:06:33.975
Part b.

00:06:34.015 --> 00:06:40.193
Methane gas plus two oxygen gas gives
carbon dioxide gas plus two liquid water,

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:46.725
with delta H equal to negative eight hundred
and ninety kilojoules for the equation

00:06:46.765 --> 00:06:47.591
as written.

00:06:47.631 --> 00:06:51.290
One mark for the balanced
equation with states,

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:55.108
and one for the delta H
with its sign and units.

00:06:55.464 --> 00:06:56.003
Part c.

00:06:56.043 --> 00:06:59.884
Each mole of methane
needs two moles of oxygen,

00:06:59.924 --> 00:07:06.573
so zero point two two five moles of methane
needs zero point four five zero moles

00:07:06.613 --> 00:07:07.399
of oxygen.

00:07:07.439 --> 00:07:10.289
We have zero point five zero eight,

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:16.814
so oxygen is in excess and all the methane
can burn, just as the question says.

00:07:16.854 --> 00:07:21.025
The excess is zero point
zero five eight one moles.

00:07:21.065 --> 00:07:27.384
Times thirty-two point zero grams per
mole, that's one point eight six grams.

00:07:27.424 --> 00:07:32.544
One mark for the oxygen required,
and one for the excess mass.

00:07:33.531 --> 00:07:36.075
Here's a tempting wrong solution.

00:07:36.115 --> 00:07:41.557
A student treats all zero point two
five zero moles of gas as methane.

00:07:41.597 --> 00:07:44.416
Watch how one reading error spreads.

00:07:44.798 --> 00:07:49.457
Their oxygen requirement becomes
zero point five zero zero moles,

00:07:49.497 --> 00:07:54.084
so their excess shrinks to just
zero point two five eight grams.

00:07:54.124 --> 00:07:59.795
Their energy released becomes two hundred
and twenty-two point five kilojoules,

00:07:59.835 --> 00:08:03.699
and their efficiency drops
to sixty point one percent.

00:08:03.739 --> 00:08:08.510
One misread phrase has changed the
answers to parts a, c, d and e.

00:08:08.864 --> 00:08:14.732
A marking scheme may still award later
marks for a correct method applied

00:08:14.772 --> 00:08:17.403
consistently to the wrong number.

00:08:17.443 --> 00:08:23.473
But the methane mark in part a is gone,
and every answer after it is wrong.

00:08:23.513 --> 00:08:28.005
The fix is a reading habit:
whenever a gas is a mixture,

00:08:28.045 --> 00:08:31.929
find the amount of the
reacting component first.

00:08:32.864 --> 00:08:33.325
Part d.

00:08:33.365 --> 00:08:39.266
The energy released is zero point two two
five moles times eight hundred and ninety

00:08:39.306 --> 00:08:43.489
kilojoules per mole: two hundred
point two five kilojoules,

00:08:43.529 --> 00:08:45.780
or about two hundred kilojoules.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:50.289
The heat gained by the water is
eight hundred point zero grams,

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:54.154
times four point one eight
joules per gram per degree,

00:08:54.194 --> 00:08:59.165
times forty point zero degrees: one
hundred and thirty-three thousand,

00:08:59.205 --> 00:09:01.384
seven hundred and sixty joules.

00:09:01.424 --> 00:09:06.220
In kilojoules, that's one hundred
and thirty-three point seven six.

00:09:06.597 --> 00:09:07.070
Part e.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.516
Both the input and the
useful output are known,

00:09:10.556 --> 00:09:14.914
so efficiency equals the useful
output divided by the input,

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:16.820
times one hundred percent.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:22.832
One hundred and thirty-three point seven
six divided by two hundred point two five

00:09:22.872 --> 00:09:25.471
gives sixty-six point eight percent.

00:09:25.511 --> 00:09:31.556
Use the unrounded two hundred point two
five here, and round only the final answer,

00:09:31.596 --> 00:09:35.041
to three significant
figures to match the data.

00:09:35.397 --> 00:09:37.841
Why not divide by an efficiency?

00:09:37.881 --> 00:09:43.974
That inverse operation is only for finding
the input needed to deliver a target

00:09:44.014 --> 00:09:44.518
output.

00:09:44.558 --> 00:09:47.701
Here we're finding the efficiency itself.

00:09:47.741 --> 00:09:53.213
Plausibility check: the answer is
between zero and one hundred percent,

00:09:53.253 --> 00:09:57.560
and the water gained less
energy than the fuel released,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.811
as it must for a real heater.

00:09:59.851 --> 00:10:04.857
Two marks for part d, and three
for part e: the ratio, the value,

00:10:04.897 --> 00:10:06.450
and the explanation.

00:10:07.396 --> 00:10:07.937
Part f.

00:10:07.977 --> 00:10:13.830
The new carbon dioxide from combustion
is one mole per mole of methane:

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:16.319
zero point two two five moles.

00:10:16.359 --> 00:10:22.128
Add the inlet carbon dioxide, ten
percent of zero point two five zero,

00:10:22.168 --> 00:10:25.863
which is zero point zero
two five zero moles.

00:10:25.903 --> 00:10:29.514
The total is zero point
two five zero moles,

00:10:29.554 --> 00:10:33.538
which at SLC occupies six
point two zero litres.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:38.848
That equals the fuel volume, and
that's a useful plausibility check.

00:10:38.888 --> 00:10:42.785
Every molecule in the fuel,
methane or carbon dioxide,

00:10:42.825 --> 00:10:47.014
contains exactly one carbon
atom, and carbon is conserved.

00:10:47.054 --> 00:10:52.191
So the moles of carbon dioxide out
must equal the moles of fuel gas in.

00:10:52.231 --> 00:10:57.408
The water isn't counted, because
we've isolated the dry carbon dioxide.

00:10:57.796 --> 00:11:01.346
Part g asks why the methane
counts as renewable.

00:11:01.386 --> 00:11:06.956
It's renewable because of its source:
it comes from recently grown biomass,

00:11:06.996 --> 00:11:12.191
such as food or farm waste, which is
replenished on a human timescale.

00:11:12.231 --> 00:11:16.604
The methane molecule itself is
identical to fossil methane.

00:11:16.644 --> 00:11:17.444
One mark.

00:11:17.663 --> 00:11:24.312
Here's the full tally: three, two, two,
two, three, two and one, for fifteen marks.

00:11:24.352 --> 00:11:29.148
Notice how many of those marks
come from steps you can show,

00:11:29.188 --> 00:11:31.445
not just from final answers.

00:11:32.395 --> 00:11:34.326
Checkpoint: spot the error.

00:11:34.366 --> 00:11:39.511
Here are three lines from different
students' answers to Q twenty-five.

00:11:39.551 --> 00:11:41.774
Each line contains one mistake.

00:11:41.814 --> 00:11:43.640
Pause and find all three.

00:11:59.061 --> 00:12:03.636
Line one uses the volume of
air as if it were all oxygen.

00:12:03.676 --> 00:12:09.384
It should use twenty-one point zero
percent of sixty point zero litres,

00:12:09.424 --> 00:12:12.663
giving zero point five zero eight moles.

00:12:13.061 --> 00:12:16.422
Line two forgets the inlet carbon dioxide.

00:12:16.462 --> 00:12:22.939
The total is zero point two five zero
moles, which is six point two zero litres.

00:12:23.328 --> 00:12:26.708
Line three has the right
number but the wrong unit.

00:12:26.748 --> 00:12:31.268
Grams times joules per gram per
degree, times degrees, gives joules:

00:12:31.308 --> 00:12:35.961
one hundred and thirty-three thousand,
seven hundred and sixty joules,

00:12:36.001 --> 00:12:40.320
which is one hundred and thirty-three
point seven six kilojoules.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:45.416
Left uncorrected, that slip makes the
efficiency a thousand times too large,

00:12:45.456 --> 00:12:48.876
which a plausibility check
would catch immediately.

00:12:49.861 --> 00:12:53.257
Here's a variation to
test your understanding.

00:12:53.297 --> 00:12:59.232
What if the heater received only fifty
point zero litres of air instead of sixty

00:12:59.272 --> 00:13:00.054
point zero?

00:13:00.094 --> 00:13:03.340
Would all the methane
still burn completely?

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:04.650
Pause and decide.

00:13:15.061 --> 00:13:20.655
Oxygen available: twenty-one point zero
percent of fifty point zero litres is ten

00:13:20.695 --> 00:13:24.411
point five litres, or zero
point four two three moles.

00:13:24.451 --> 00:13:28.167
But the methane needs zero
point four five zero moles.

00:13:28.207 --> 00:13:33.593
Now oxygen is the limiting reactant, so
the methane can't all burn completely.

00:13:33.633 --> 00:13:37.766
Some may burn incompletely,
forming carbon monoxide or soot,

00:13:37.806 --> 00:13:39.714
and less energy is released.

00:13:39.754 --> 00:13:44.693
Always check which reactant limits
before assuming complete combustion.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:49.398
Practice question Q twenty-six
is worth ten marks.

00:13:49.438 --> 00:13:53.327
It compares a methanol heater
and an ethanol heater,

00:13:53.367 --> 00:13:56.123
and the ranking depends on the basis.

00:13:56.163 --> 00:13:57.372
Read it through.

00:13:57.726 --> 00:14:01.095
Pause the video now and
attempt all four parts.

00:14:01.135 --> 00:14:05.157
If you keep it running, here's
some quiet thinking time.

00:14:05.197 --> 00:14:10.960
Before you calculate anything, decide what
"per useful kilojoule" means for each

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:11.800
heater.

00:14:57.526 --> 00:14:58.610
Annotate first.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:00.706
The enthalpies are per mole.

00:15:00.746 --> 00:15:04.002
The two heaters have
different efficiencies.

00:15:04.042 --> 00:15:08.870
And part b asks for grams of carbon
dioxide per useful kilojoule,

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:12.729
which sets the basis for
every number in that part.

00:15:13.126 --> 00:15:13.565
Part a.

00:15:13.605 --> 00:15:18.623
Methanol: seven hundred and twenty-six
divided by thirty-two point zero is

00:15:18.663 --> 00:15:21.562
twenty-two point seven
kilojoules per gram.

00:15:21.602 --> 00:15:27.167
Ethanol: one thousand three hundred and
seventy divided by forty-six point zero is

00:15:27.207 --> 00:15:30.175
twenty-nine point eight
kilojoules per gram.

00:15:30.215 --> 00:15:31.172
One mark each.

00:15:31.526 --> 00:15:31.997
Part b.

00:15:32.037 --> 00:15:33.604
Work per mole of fuel.

00:15:33.644 --> 00:15:39.665
One mole of methanol gives one mole of
carbon dioxide, forty-four point zero grams,

00:15:39.705 --> 00:15:44.995
and delivers seven hundred and twenty-six
times zero point two five zero:

00:15:45.035 --> 00:15:49.814
one hundred and eighty-one point
five kilojoules of useful energy.

00:15:49.854 --> 00:15:55.510
Forty-four point zero divided by one
hundred and eighty-one point five is zero

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.909
point two four two grams
per useful kilojoule.

00:15:59.259 --> 00:16:03.031
One mole of ethanol gives
two moles of carbon dioxide,

00:16:03.071 --> 00:16:08.819
eighty-eight point zero grams, and delivers
one thousand three hundred and seventy

00:16:08.859 --> 00:16:14.607
times zero point four zero zero: five
hundred and forty-eight kilojoules of useful

00:16:14.647 --> 00:16:15.101
energy.

00:16:15.141 --> 00:16:20.819
Eighty-eight point zero divided by five
hundred and forty-eight is zero point one

00:16:20.859 --> 00:16:23.290
six one grams per useful kilojoule.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:29.078
So the ethanol heater emits less direct
carbon dioxide for the same useful energy.

00:16:29.118 --> 00:16:34.270
Four marks: one for each useful energy,
and one for each emission figure.

00:16:34.659 --> 00:16:35.931
Is that plausible?

00:16:35.971 --> 00:16:39.574
Per kilojoule released,
the two fuels are similar:

00:16:39.614 --> 00:16:44.601
about zero point zero six grams of
carbon dioxide per kilojoule each.

00:16:44.641 --> 00:16:48.317
So the difference must come
mostly from efficiency,

00:16:48.357 --> 00:16:53.271
forty percent versus twenty-five
percent, a factor of one point six.

00:16:53.311 --> 00:16:56.663
Our ratio is about one
point five, which fits.

00:16:57.659 --> 00:16:59.693
Now a tempting wrong solution.

00:16:59.733 --> 00:17:02.458
A student calculates methanol correctly,

00:17:02.498 --> 00:17:06.191
at zero point two four two
grams per useful kilojoule.

00:17:06.231 --> 00:17:11.860
But for ethanol they divide eighty-eight
point zero grams by the full one thousand

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:15.039
three hundred and seventy
kilojoules released,

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.597
getting zero point zero six four two,

00:17:17.637 --> 00:17:21.785
and they conclude that ethanol
emits nearly four times less.

00:17:22.126 --> 00:17:24.909
The two numbers are on different bases.

00:17:24.949 --> 00:17:29.615
One is per useful kilojoule; the
other is per kilojoule released,

00:17:29.655 --> 00:17:32.366
which ignores the heater's efficiency.

00:17:32.406 --> 00:17:35.624
Compared properly, both
per useful kilojoule,

00:17:35.664 --> 00:17:40.225
ethanol is lower by a factor of
about one point five, not four.

00:17:40.592 --> 00:17:46.417
Another version of the same trap compares
carbon dioxide per mole of fuel:

00:17:46.457 --> 00:17:52.441
one mole for methanol and two for ethanol,
so "methanol emits half as much".

00:17:52.481 --> 00:17:58.544
That's true per mole of fuel, but a mole
of methanol delivers far less useful

00:17:58.584 --> 00:17:59.099
energy.

00:17:59.139 --> 00:18:03.657
Always put both fuels on the
basis the question asks for.

00:18:04.592 --> 00:18:05.116
Part c.

00:18:05.156 --> 00:18:10.038
The claim is that both fuels
contain an oxygen hydrogen bond,

00:18:10.078 --> 00:18:13.103
so they release equal energy per gram.

00:18:13.143 --> 00:18:15.443
That's wrong for two reasons.

00:18:15.483 --> 00:18:21.494
The energy released depends on all the
bonds broken and formed in the whole

00:18:21.534 --> 00:18:24.479
reaction, not on one bond they share.

00:18:24.519 --> 00:18:28.190
And per-gram values also
depend on molar mass.

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:30.085
One mark for each idea.

00:18:30.458 --> 00:18:30.931
Part d.

00:18:30.971 --> 00:18:35.542
Do these results show which fuel
has lower lifecycle emissions?

00:18:35.582 --> 00:18:35.762
No.

00:18:35.802 --> 00:18:40.227
We've only counted the direct
carbon dioxide from combustion.

00:18:40.267 --> 00:18:44.400
Lifecycle emissions also
include producing the feedstock,

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:50.037
processing the fuel and transporting
it, and we have no data on any of those.

00:18:50.077 --> 00:18:53.883
One mark for the conclusion,
and one for the reason.

00:18:54.258 --> 00:18:59.170
The full tally: two, four,
two and two, for ten marks.

00:19:00.125 --> 00:19:03.155
Before we finish, start an error log.

00:19:03.195 --> 00:19:08.964
For each mistake you made today,
write one row: the question and part,

00:19:09.004 --> 00:19:15.975
what you did, the type of error, the correct
idea, and a check you'll use next time.

00:19:16.325 --> 00:19:19.197
Here's an example entry you can copy.

00:19:19.237 --> 00:19:20.929
Q twenty-five, part a.

00:19:20.969 --> 00:19:25.888
What I did: used zero point two
five zero moles as the methane.

00:19:25.928 --> 00:19:30.217
Type of error: reading, a
mixture versus its component.

00:19:30.257 --> 00:19:34.310
Correct idea: multiply by
the volume fraction first.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:40.883
Next time: circle every percentage in a
gas question and ask, "percentage of what?"

00:19:41.258 --> 00:19:42.063
Your turn.

00:19:42.103 --> 00:19:46.583
Pause and write at least
one entry from today's work.

00:20:06.991 --> 00:20:12.331
To recap: read and annotate, choose the
method, show every quantity with units,

00:20:12.371 --> 00:20:13.898
and check plausibility.

00:20:13.938 --> 00:20:18.733
Watch for mixtures, air versus oxygen,
inlet versus new carbon dioxide,

00:20:18.773 --> 00:20:21.048
and the basis of every comparison.

00:20:21.088 --> 00:20:26.332
Next episode is our second exam workshop,
on calorimetry and data evaluation.
